se can be anything from pod security  policies to  firewall policies anything that  describes the behavior to essentially  provide guardrails to  application teams is what we define as a  policy  now these are ultimately stored directly  as kubernetes objects  and are handled by amp ampdos  configuration management  so under the hood anthous configuration  management is listening to that kit  repository  and allows our security teams in  coordination with our operations teams  and development teams  to push those policies and  configurations acm takes care of the  rest  taking those configurations and properly  propagating them  across those clusters allowing for full  code review  continuous integration and full  integration into  development now ecosystem just a little  bit about how that works i'm going to  hand it over to ben  thanks brandon let's take a look at the  demo  let's take a look at a couple of the  workflows that brandon was just  describing  in the cicd on anthos presentation  so before we dig into the exact into the  workflows let's take a look at the gke  clusters  that we have deployed and what we'll be  using them for  so we have four clusters that we'll be  using to run  our applications the first is a dev  cluster  so in the demo we'll use scaffold to  automatically detect the changes to the  application code and then  deploy them into the dev cluster this  will enable us to live test  our changes on the dev cluster before we  push them into the repository  from there once when they're pushed into  the main line of the application code  repository a deployment is automatically  kicked off  which deploys our changes to the staging  cluster  then once when we validated our changes  in the staging cluster  we can merge the environment  configuration  from the staging branch into the  mainline branch  which would trigger the production  deployment to our two production  clusters  uh the fifth cluster that we have out  here is used for  shared services so on that cluster we're  running git lab  and that's where we'll be storing our  source code and running our cacd  pipelines  so now let's pretend that i'm an  application developer  and i have a simple go application if we  take a look at that  we see that it simply outputs hello  world  and my task is to update  that reading this output from hello  world to something else  so as a as a good developer what we'll  do is we'll start by creating a new  branch  we'll call it new greeting  and before i start making changes here i  want to  get scaffold started so it's connected  up to our development cluster  let's first make sure that we're in the  right context here  there you go  so scaffold is going to watch our source  code  and as it changes it's automatically  going to build new versions of the  container  push them into artifact registry and  then run  deployments on those of those new  container images out to our deployment  our development cluster so that way we  can test  so we can see here that scaffold is up  and running  and if we preview  we can see hello world is output so now  we're ready to get started with our  changes  so we'll go to our editor here  so we're going to say hello google  on air so that'll be our new greeting  so we can see as i've changed back to  cloud shell that scaffold has detected  that change and it's already starting to  rebuild the image  okay now the image has been deployed so  if we refresh  in our browser ah there's our new  greeting  and that looks good so we're ready to  push our changes  into our source control system and  kick off the cacd pipelines  so let's stop scaffold here  and we'll commit our changes  and then we'll push them out  okay so now if we go over into  git lab  and we log in as the developer here  me in this case  okay we can see that i just pushed a new  branch so let's go ahead and create a  merge request  so at this point what's happening is  we're running a ci pipeline  on our branch and we are tech  running tests we're gonna hydrate the  manifests  and we're gonna validate the manifest as  well so we're running  uh a subset of the stages that will run  later on so everything except for the  deployment part of the pipeline  so this is giving us the opportunity to  validate any manifest changes that we  might have made  in the application code repository as  well as any application code changes  and we'll just have git lab merge when  it succeeds  okay so we can see through the comments  here that  everything is merged  so if we look at our ci cd pipelines  we're now running  another pipeline so in this one we're  running tests again  we're gonna build our new image  and we're going to hydrate and validate  configs  and then we're going to push those  configs into our environment repository  so while we wait for this to run let's  take a quick look  at our repository and what we have in  here  so in here we have our source code so  our main.go  with our greeting we have our docker  file and then we have  some kubernetes manifest so these  manifests are the changes that we want  to make  so in this case we're setting the  environment to dev  for each environment and if we look at  staging  or production we can see that we're also  setting the environment to prod  so these changes are getting merged  together  with the common shared best practices  that are stored  in this shared customized basis so  here's our shared basis and these  this is a full or manifest so  those environment settings are getting  merged into this  and what the ci pipeline the ci cd  pipeline is doing  is it is going to commit those manifests  push those manifests into the staging  branch here  so it's still running at this point  okay so our pipeline is now finished  so if we go and look at our environment  repository  in our staging branch we can see that we  just  pushed new configs both staging and  production animals  we hydrate them all at the same time so  they're consistent  and see they are all fully fully  hydrated  manifests  so what that push did is that push  triggered a pipeline to deploy those  manifests  to our staging kubernetes cluster  that's now done so if we switch back to  cloud shell  now we can see what we have running in  our namespace  and our application  is now running and it's been running for  24 seconds so we have deployed our  changes  to our staging cluster now we'll quickly  promote those changes from the staging  branch into the mainline branch and that  will trigger the production deployment  so we'll go in here we'll make a new  merge request  okay we'll merge those  if we take a quick peek at the changes  here we can see that all we really  changed  was the hash  but that merge request has now triggered  the deployment to production so  we'll switch to our prod central context  and we can see that our new pods have  been running for about 14 seconds  so our deployment to our production  cluster is succeeded  now for the next workflow um as the  developer i've been talking with  the uh hipster shop front-end team that  they also have their application running  on our cluster  and they would like to be able to  connect to our  go application that we've been working  with so  what we're going to do is we're going to  switch over to the anthos config  management  repository so you can see  here we have all of our namespaces and  our cluster registries and that kind of  stuff  in there so what we're going to do is  we're going to make  a quick change to our network policy uh  to allow hipster shot front end to  connect to  our go app so  first before we get started here let's  take a quick look at our  current network policies  so we can see that right here we have  a uh just a default deny  for anything that's not within our  namespace so  and what we're going to do is we're  going to enable that  for hipster the hipster shot front end  so let's start again by creating a  branch  and we'll jump back over to our editor  and then here's our network policy that  is sitting inside of our namespaces and  our managed applications  so our go app  so this policy is going to allow traffic  from the hipster  front end to connect to our go  application  so we'll save that  okay and then let's push our change  okay so our change has been pushed so if  we jump back over to the get lab ui here  what we'll see is we just pushed  a new branch so since i pushed that  branch as the developer i'm going to  create a merge request  so we're going to allow traffic from the  hipster front end  so we'll submit that merge request now  uh our policy has been set up such that  i  am not as a developer i'm not allowed to  merge or to merge requests into  the main line so we're going to have to  uh  switch over to our network admin so now  i'm going to be our network  administrator  and we'll review those policy changes so  that way we can  merge that request in and allow that  traffic and while we're doing that you  can see that we've connect  we've kicked off another ci pipeline  where we're verifying  uh the changes that we're making to our  configuration  so we're running ci on our config on our  anthos configurations  let's switch over  and we have a merge request here so now  i'm in is the network administrator  and we see the merge request here from  me  uh wanting to allow traffic from the  hipster front end  into our go application so our while we  were doing that all of our test  pass so let's quickly view our changes  so there's our changes that we made look  good  so let's merge them  okay and now we're going to run ci  on them again just to make sure  everything is still good  and once when ci has finished we'll be  able to go out to our cluster and we'll  see that this  change has been applied and we'll check  it on both the production and staging  cluster  okay ci is finished so now if we jump  back  out to cloud shell and we look at our  network policies again  now we see that we're allowing traffic  from  application hipster front-end into our  into our go app namespace so  that process as a developer i needed to  make a network policy change i  made the change in the anthos  configuration management  repository and then that change was  reviewed and approved by a network  administrator  as we saw in the demo we had several  applications deployed  across a number of clusters both  pre-production and production  each application ran in their own  namespace or their own little playground  so that way they ran in an  isolated environment in order to allow  applications to communicate with each  other we had to involve  the operations and security team so that  way we could update the network policies  for those namespaces  and those policies and configurations  were being managed using  acm automatically being deployed and  reconciled across  all the clusters  now as a developer they can focus on  using a get ops workflow  so developers can build new features in  future branches and using tools like  scaffold  those changes can be automatically  deployed to a development cluster  so they can be live tested  when those changes are finished a merge  request is created  and when they're merged into the main  line the staging deployment is  automatically triggered by the  push of hydrated manifest into the  staging branch  of the environment repository  after those changes have been validated  on this aging cluster  the operations team or the developer  depending on your workflow can create a  merge request  to promote the configurations from the  staging branch  to the mainline branch and when those  changes are merged  to the main line the deployment to  production is automatically  triggered  so using the principles that we  described you are now  able to build upon a secure  platform where you're creating and  deploying your policy  using anthos configuration manager  you're storing that configuration as  code  so you automatically get the tracking  and traceability  provided by git and as you saw in the  demo  everything is highly automated from the  deployment of application changes  all the way through the configuration  changes that are  tested and deployed using acm  we're also using containers so we're  leveraging the immutable nature of  containers to increase the  reproducibility of our deployments  in our infrastructure and we're  leveraging anthos  and kubernetes as an open source  standards-based  infrastructure that you can then extend  your infrastructure  even further  brandon and i would like to thank you  for your time today
 

